  "content": {
    "id": "p15-q7-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "7",
    "prompt": "There are infinitely many parabolas that pass through the points $(0,0)$ and $(1,1)$. Find a general equation that describes all such parabolas.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": {
        "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
        "version": 1,
        "blocks": [
          {
            "id": "p15-q7-short-result",
            "type": "paragraph",
            "fontSize": null,
            "align": "left",
            "spaceBefore": 0,
            "spaceAfter": 2,
            "lineHeight": 1.4,
            "indent": 0,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "type": "math",
                "latex": "y=ax^2+(1-a)x,\\quad a\\ne0",
                "display": false
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "p15-q7-short-method",
            "type": "paragraph",
            "fontSize": null,
            "align": "left",
            "spaceBefore": 0,
            "spaceAfter": 2,
            "lineHeight": 1.4,
            "indent": 0,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Substitute the two points into the general quadratic.",
                "marks": []
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      "worked": "Let $y=ax^2+bx+c$, where $a\\ne0$.\n\nSubstituting $(0,0)$ gives\n$$\\begin{align*}0&=a(0)^2+b(0)+c\\\\c&=0.\\end{align*}$$\nSubstituting $(1,1)$ gives\n$$\\begin{align*}1&=a(1)^2+b(1)\\\\1&=a+b\\\\b&=1-a.\\end{align*}$$\nThus all such parabolas have equation $y=ax^2+(1-a)x$, where $a\\ne0$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 45
  },
